From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 11:34:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: keep a kernel mapping of VE buffers on
 ARM32

Buffers that only the VE accesses were allocated with
DMA_ATTR_NO_KERNEL_MAPPING. dma-direct then returns the pages through
dma_direct_alloc_no_mapping(), which relies on arch_dma_prep_coherent() to
remove dirty lines from the kernel's cacheable linear alias. ARM32 does not
select ARCH_HAS_DMA_PREP_COHERENT, so the call is an empty stub, and the
ARM allocator that would clean the alias (arch_dma_alloc) is bypassed.
Lines left dirty there - e.g. by init_on_alloc zeroing the CMA pages
through the cached alias - are evicted later, over data the VE has
written in the meantime.

On the A20 (VE 0x1623) this corrupted the H.264 co-located motion vectors
(MV-col buffers): B frames decoded with displaced blocks, differently on
every run, until the stale lines were gone (Big Buck Bunny 1080p: 52/300
frames bit-exact, Y-PSNR 39-55 dB). Giving only the MV-col buffers a
kernel mapping made them 300/300 bit-exact; so did keeping
NO_KERNEL_MAPPING but invalidating (discarding, not writing back) their
linear alias before every job, which rules out the VE merely reading
memory that was never zeroed.

Use CEDRUS_HW_BUF_ATTRS for all VE-only buffers and the OUTPUT queue: no
kernel mapping only where the architecture implements
arch_dma_prep_coherent(). With a kernel mapping, the ARM32 allocator
flushes the alias and remaps it non-cacheable; CMA is in lowmem on these
SoCs, so this costs no vmalloc space.

Result (A20, VDPAU and GStreamer): Big Buck Bunny 1080p 300/300 frames
bit-exact against FFmpeg's software decoder, identical on every run. The
sporadic decode error on the first frame of a stream (8/100 runs with
GStreamer) also disappeared (0/100); which buffer caused it was not
isolated. The encoder's buffers use the same attributes now (same
allocation pattern; its regression suite passes).

The proper fix belongs in arch/arm or dma-direct (an ARM32
arch_dma_prep_coherent(), or not taking the no-mapping shortcut for
non-coherent devices on ARCH_HAS_DMA_ALLOC architectures); this keeps the
driver safe until then.

Tested on Cubieboard2 and Cubietruck (A20).

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
